// table-driven relation edge cases:
// a longer keyword sequence that fails to complete falls back to a
// shorter one (the ranked-row retry)
x is a partition
x is a relation
// (every relation consumes exactly one step, and a
// dangling relation keyword is a parse error rather than a symbol LC -
// those locks live in LurchParserErrorTests, as do the n-ary
// runs of the single-step rows: H subgroup G sqsubseteq K,
// A normal B ⊴ C, a ~ b ~ c, a rel b rel c are parse errors)
// mixed ⊆ aliases share one chain family
A subset B subseteq C
// naked bindings are not relation arguments: a binding
// operand must be parenthesized to keep its scope legible (the naked
// forms are locked in LurchParserErrorTests)
(x.f(x)) ~ (y.f(y))
a ~ (x.y)
// 'normal' classifies as an operator word, so it works in the bare
// mention holes like any other operator
normal is not transitive
// the one word-boundary rule: word keywords end at word
// boundaries, so relx/simx are ordinary symbols; and a word keyword may
// not directly abut '(' - a name followed by '(' with
// no space reads as function application in Lurch, so these split into
// an operand plus a mention-headed application (visibly different from
// the spaced infix forms below them)
a relx b
a simx b
a is(b)
a sim(b)
x leq(y)
n divides(n+1)*n
n divides (n+1)*n
// sim no longer needs whitespace on its left, so a digit boundary works
// exactly as it does for leq
2sim 3
(a)in b
(a)is b
// operator words remain mentionable symbols in relation operands
loves is a relation on people
is is weird
// a keyword choice that matched inside ONE old rule is committed: when
// the right-hand side after 'is a' fails, there is NO fallback to bare
// 'is' - fallback exists only across rows, like the
// partition row falling back to the is row here (the
// committed-and-failed shapes are parse errors, locked in
// LurchParserErrorTests)
x is a partition, P
// table-driven precedence-climb edge
// cases: same-level runs of a flat row flatten n-ary, even across
// tighter operators in between (an unparenthesized iff run is an
// error - see LurchParserErrorTests)
