A cup B cap C cup D
a + b - c + d
a cup b cup c cap d cap e
// delimited big-operator
// call forms (and `int f dx`) are closed units, valid in every hole -
// including the star holes and the unary - and / operands - and the English
// indexed-set forms are ordinary operand units of every row
sum(f,k,1,n) star x
x star sum(f,k,1,n)
- sum(f,k,1,n)
/sum(f,k,1,n)
int x^2 dx star y
Union(B,k,A) cdot x
Union(B,k,A) + x
x cdot Union k in A of B
x + Union k in A of B
// closed calls are tight units:
// they take the postfix factorial and serve as exponentiation bases
sum(f,k,1,n)!
sum(f,k,1,n)^2
product(f,k,1,n)^2
product(f,k,1,n)^2!
Union(B,k,A)^2
int f dx ^ 2
sum(f,k,1,n)'
sum(f,k,1,n)^2'
// the closed-unit schema: every closed form takes
// the postfixes and serves as an exponent base EXCEPT application
// results, so the delimited Prefix call forms, the index bracket, and
// all postfix results join the big-operator calls above (f(x)^2 and
// @P(k)^2 alone stay targeted errors, and exponents stay Atomic - see
// LurchParserErrorTests)
set(1,2)^2
tuple(1,2)^2
paren(x+1)^2
[G:H]^2
x!!
A''
x!'
x!^2
A'^2
f(x)!^2
sum(f,k,1,n)!^2
x^2!'
// the param-aware climb: a star-family token may carry a
// subscripted parameter group, applying the compound operator, and a run
// continues only on structurally equal parameters (left-nested, like the
// plain star family); mismatched parameters end the expression and the
// leftover junk-splits via the subscripted mention, the same locked
// leftover class as mismatched chain moduli
a oplus_(n) b
x star_(G) y
a ⊕_(n) b ⊕_(n) c
a ⊕_(n+1) b ⊕_( n + 1 ) c
a ⊕_(n) b ⊕_(m) c
// prop connectives with the grammar-level prefix not
not P and Q
P and not forall x. Q
(P)iff Q
// a quantifier is allowed as a connective operand with maximal-scope
// body: the body extends rightward to the enclosing delimiter
P implies exists y. Q
P implies exists y. Q and R
P iff exists y. Q implies R
0 < x implies exists y. x < y
forall x in RR. 0 < x implies exists y. x < y
// (a same-level token that cannot continue the run still ends the
// expression - choose is not chainable, the star family shares one
// level - the dangling leftovers are parse errors,
// locked in LurchParserErrorTests)
// a tighter operator in between opens a fresh same-level run
a choose b circ c choose d
a star b cdot c oplus d
// the greedy-right asymmetry of the old ladder: composition takes a
// full algebraic right operand, so it binds looser than + on the right
A circ B + C
A + B circ C
// operand classes: the star family takes only the tight units
a star b factorial
a factorial star b
- a star b
// the indexed-set forms take only setminus..circ on either side (the
// violating compositions are parse errors, locked in
// LurchParserErrorTests)
Union(B,k,A) circ g
Union(B,k,A) cap C
// the sum and int big operators take ⋅ and looser but never the star
// level (product(...) is an ordinary Prefix unit and stars fine)
sum(f,k,1,n) cdot 2
sum(f,k,1,n) choose 2
product(f,k,1,n) star x
// a delimited summation is a closed unit and composes like any other
// operand on both sides, so these parse as ordinary sums
1 + sum(f,k,1,n) cdot 2
2 cdot sum(f,k,1,n) + 1
sum(f,k,1,n) cdot 2 + 1
a choose sum(f,k,1,n) cdot 2 + 1
// the multi-hole mixfix patterns (maps and the English congruence)
// are table rows resolved by the same ranked machinery as the
// single-keyword relations
f : A cup B to C cross D
g circ f : A to C
f(x) : A to B
f_(0) : NN to A
(g circ f) : A to B
forall f. f : A to B
f : A to B iff P
f : A to B, g : B to C
a CONG b mod m
a cong (b+1) mod (m cdot 2)
P and a cong b mod m
a cong b mod m by transitivity
// call-form bodies are AlgExpr for
// every operator, the over-a-set keyword regimes are uniform (the
// English interior keywords are case-sensitive lowercase and spaced on
// both sides), and leading names are case-insensitive
int(x+y,k,1,n)
sum(x+y,k,1,n)
product(x+y,k,1,n)
Union x+y for k in S
Union k in (S) of B
sum k in (A union B) of k
sum f for k in (S)
sum k=2 to 5 of 3*k
product(sum(f,k,1,n),j,1,m)
Union k in A of Intersect j in J of B
int f dx cdot 2
Product(f,k,1,n)
DEFINT(f,x,a,b)
integral(f,k,1,n)
// (defint outside its 4-argument Algebrite call form, and the
// wrt forms, are parse errors - locked in
// LurchParserErrorTests - instead of silently degrading to function
// applications and symbol LCs)
// opaque tokenizer segments: «» escapes, "string literals", and
// // comments pass through input normalization verbatim - no comma/colon
// padding, space shrinking, phrase/unicode replacement, or division
// rewriting inside them - and a unicode operator BETWEEN two strings on
// one line is no longer mistaken for string content
«[a,b]»
% "a,b  with commas, doubled  spaces, and for all phrases"
% "a" P∧Q % "b"
x = y // trans, and/or  note

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Token classification, the uniform
// mention rule, the bare-mention holes, the ≈ relation
// row, and the keyword-case sweep.  (The dangling-operator junk-parses
// that used to end this file are parse errors now, locked in
// LurchParserErrorTests.)

// ≈ is a generic relation like ~ (single-step since the chain-operator
// table: a ≈ b ≈ c is a parse error, locked in LurchParserErrorTests);
// the glyph reaches the grammar as 'approx' and the canonical head is ≈
x ≈ y
a approx b
'≈' is reflexive
Declare ~, ≈, reflexive
// the uniform mention rule: an operator name alone in parentheses or
// single quotes denotes the operator symbol, for every alias (quotes and
// parens produce the same atom)
(subset)
'star'
(+)
(=)
'≈' subset A cross A
as + 1
// bare-mention holes: application heads and lone arguments accept an
// operator name with no marker (all three head forms agree), and so do
// tuple and set elements - the algebraic-structure tuple names its
// operation bare ...
[G,star,e]
[a,~]
⟨G,star,e⟩
set(star,cdot)
oplus(2,3)
⊕(2,3)
'⊕'(2,3)
(oplus)(2,3)
and(P,Q)
loves(x,y)
P_(~)
// ... as do the first operand of any relation ...
~ is a relation on A
~ subset A cross A
subset is transitive
// ... the ∈ right-hand side ...
x in cap
⊕ in S
// negated English verbs (note English drops the s), and 'proves' now
// renders as English text in tex while vdash/⊢ stay the turnstile
a does not love b
Dracula does not fear Alice
a does not prove b
// ... and declared-name and bound-variable slots
Let otimes
Let star be such that x star y = y star x
forall otimes. x otimes y = y otimes x
oplus.k
// phrase replacement now needs word boundaries on BOTH sides: 'xfor
// ally' is two symbols, not a corrupted forall, and 'for allz.T(z)'
// (missing space) is a parse error, locked in LurchParserErrorTests
xfor ally
// uppercase variants of the CS-lowercase interior keywords are ordinary
// symbols (the keyword-case policy pollutes no more of the symbol space
// than the keywords themselves), so these parse - as junk symbol LCs
Let x IN A
Let x BE SUCH THAT x in A

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// parameterized infix relations - a op_(params) b applies
// the compound operator (op params...),
// for op among ~, approx, rel, and =; congruence mod m is the
// parameterized ((≅ m) a b) with four synonymous surfaces (since the
// cong-chains slice the cong_(m) surface is a chain step - see the
// section below - whose single-step output is byte-identical)
a cong_(m) b
a = b mod m
a=b mod m
x =_(0) y
a approx_(p) b
x rel_(r) y
a ≈ _(p) b
// 'mod' is CS lowercase and 'Mod' an ordinary symbol, so these parse as
// an equation plus symbol LCs, not congruences
a = b Mod m
a = b mod

// times → × and are → is live in the internal-name
// table (Declare/mention agreement), so Declares of those words declare
// the operators, like every other alias
Declare times, are

// the quoted mention of the division glyph works like every
// other glyph mention, and dividing by a quoted mention is
// still a division
'/'
(/)
x / '~'
x/'~'

// a mention of cong is the operator ≅ (renders \cong in tex)
(cong)

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// cong_(m) is a transitive-chain operator.
// A chain mixes = (neutral) with ONE operator family - the inequality
// family =/</≤ or the congruence family =/cong_(m) - committing at its
// first non-= step; the single-step case prints ((≅ m) a b), exactly
// the old ParamRel output, and the English 'a cong b mod m' remains
// deliberately non-chaining
a cong_(m) b cong_(m) c
a = b cong_(m) c
a cong_(m) b = c
a = b cong_(m) c = d cong_(m) e
2 cong_(2) 0 cong_(2) 4
a+1 cong_(m) b*2 cong_(m) c^2
a ≅_(m) b ≅_(m) c
a cong_(m) b cong_(m) c by test
not a cong_(m) b
forall x. x cong_(m) y cong_(m) z
x in set(k : k cong_(m) a = j)
// a congruence chain also commits to its MODULUS: a cong
// step with structurally different parameters ends the chain like a
// cross-family token, so a chain's transitive conclusion always exists
// (congruences compose only when the moduli agree).  The leftover
// parses as the subscripted mention plus its operand - the expression
// dialog's chain gate is what refuses trans_chain-plus-leftovers input.
// The comparison strips fmt and parens, so cong_( m ) continues a
// cong_(m) chain but cong_(1+1) does not continue a cong_(2) chain.
a cong_(m) b cong_(k) c
a cong_(m) b cong_( m ) c
a cong_(2) b cong_(1+1) c
a cong_(m,k) b cong_(m,k) c
a cong_(m,k) b cong_(k,m) c
// a congruence token after a committed inequality chain ends the chain,
// and the leftover cong_(m) still parses as the subscripted mention
// (≅ m) - the one pre-existing junk-split this slice does not remove,
// byte-identical to the pre-slice parse (the cong-committed direction
// is a targeted error; see the error suite)
a = b < c cong_(m) d

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// The chain-operator
// table: families (<,≤,=), (cong_(m),=), (≅,=), (|,=), (⊆,=).
// The | and ⊆ rows live in the chain machinery with
// unchanged single-step output, and bare congruence chains too: the
// geometer's `a cong b` is (≅ a b).
// single-step chains print exactly what the relation rows printed
m | n
m divides n
A subset B
A ⊆ B
a cong b
// multi-step chains per family, = neutral throughout
a | b | c
m divides n divides p
a = b | c
a | b = c
A subset B = C
A = B subset C
a cong b cong c
a = b cong c
a cong b = c
a cong b cong c by test
// chain steps carry by-reasons like every chain family
m | n by arithmetic
A subset B subset C by transitivity
// a bare operator mention may open a | or ⊆ chain (the old
// BareRelation reachability, preserved when the rows moved)
~ | b
~ subset A cross A
~ subset A subset B
// cross-family tokens end the chain; the leftover here parses as the
// subscripted mention (≅ m) plus its operand, exactly like the
// ineq-then-cong_(m) leftover locked above
a cong b cong_(m) c
// bare cong is an operator like =, so the junk-absorptions = permits
// are permitted verbatim for it (each is byte-parallel to the same
// input with = in place of cong - the uppercase MOD/TO keyword-case
// locks and the naked-binding | lock below.  The bindings
// 'a cong x.y mod c' / 'a cong x mapsto y mod c' junk-parse the same
// way but are locked nowhere: their canonical reprints rejoin across
// line breaks into the English congruence row, so they cannot live in
// the round-tripped line corpus.)
a cong b MOD m
a cong mod b TO c
a | x.y

// ('a is a' does not belong here: 'is a' commits with no
// right-hand side, a parse error locked in LurchParserErrorTests)
